----------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\) with \\(n\\ge 4\\).\nAll points of the diagonal \\(D_n\\) must be covered.  \nBecause any two points of \\(D_n\\) determine a line of slope \\(-1\\), which is not sunny, each sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(D_n\\). Hence at least \\(|D_n|=n\\) sunny lines would be required, i.e. all \\(n\\) lines would have to be sunny.  \n\nPick the point \\((1,1)\\in S_n\\). Since the line through \\((1,1)\\) with slope \\(1\\) is sunny, we may assume that one of the \\(n\\) lines is the line \\(y=x\\) (otherwise \\((1,1)\\) would have to lie on a non‑sunny line, which is impossible because a non‑sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) would be either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); none of those passes through \\((1,1)\\) together with another point of \\(S_n\\). Consequently the line \\(y=x\\) is forced to be present. The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines must cover the \\(n-1\\) points of \\(D_n\\setminus\\{(1,n)\\}\\). As argued above, a sunny line can meet at most one of those points, so we would need at least \\(n-1\\) additional sunny lines, i.e. all \\(n\\) lines would have to be sunny, which we already have. No contradiction yet. However the line \\(y=x\\) already contains the point \\((1,1)\\) and also the point \\((2,2)\\) (which belongs to \\(D_n\\) only when \\(n=3\\)). For \\(n\\ge4\\) the point \\((2,2)\\) is **not** on the diagonal, so it must be covered by some line different from \\(y=x\\). That line cannot be vertical or horizontal (they would miss many points), and it cannot have slope \\(-1\\). Hence it must be sunny, giving at least two sunny lines that contain points of \\(D_n\\). Repeating the argument shows that at least two points of \\(D_n\\) would have to lie on the same sunny line, which is impossible. Hence a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sun