ying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   Explicit families of three distinct lines are exhibited for the four possible values  \n   \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).  \n2. **Inductive step.**  \n   Assume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) we already have, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   From such a configuration we build a configuration for \\(n+1\\) lines as follows.  \n\n   *To keep the number of sunny lines unchanged* (\\(k\\le n\\)): add the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\).  \n   This line is not sunny, it is distinct from the previous ones, and it covers the only new lattice point \\((n+1,1)\\) that appears when the bound changes from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) to \\(a+b\\le n+2\\).\n\n   *To increase the number of sunny lines by one* (\\(k=n+1\\)): add a sunny line through the new point \\((n+1,1)\\); for instance the line \\(y=x-n\\) (slope \\(1\\), which is allowed).  \n   The line is sunny, distinct from all previous lines, and again covers the only new point.\n\n   In both cases the covering property for all points with \\(a+b\\le n+2\\) is preserved.\n\n3. By induction the statement holds for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n\nThe construction shows that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised, and no other value is possible because the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]