f sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe following families of three distinct lines satisfy the required covering and contain exactly the indicated number of sunny lines.\n\n* \\(k=0\\) (no sunny line).  \n  \\[\n  L_1:x=1,\\qquad L_2:x=2,\\qquad L_3:x=3 .\n  \\]\n\n* \\(k=1\\) (one sunny line).  \n  \\[\n  L_1:x=1,\\qquad L_2:x=2,\\qquad L_3:y=\\tfrac12x-\\tfrac12 .\n  \\]\n  The third line has slope \\(\\frac12\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); it passes through \\((3,1)\\).\n\n* \\(k=2\\) (two sunny lines).  \n  \\[\n  L_1:y=1\\quad(\\text{horizontal, not sunny}),\\qquad \n  L_2:y=\\tfrac12x-\\tfrac12,\\qquad \n  L_3:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 .\n  \\]\n  The two slant lines have slopes \\(\\pm\\frac12\\), both admissible, and together they cover the remaining points.\n\n* \\(k=3\\) (three sunny lines).  \n  \\[\n  L_t:\\; y=\\frac{t}{t+1}x+\\frac{1}{t+1}\\qquad (t=1,2,3).\n  \\]\n  For each \\(t\\) the slope \\(\\frac{t}{t+1}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); a direct check shows that every \\((a,b)\\in S_3\\) lies on the line with \\(t=a\\).\n\nThus the statement is true for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Inductive construction\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there exists a set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* each point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\);\