            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nFix a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(S_n\\).  \nFor a fixed column \\(x=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\)) denote by \\(c_i\\) the number of lines of the configuration that are **not** vertical and intersect the column \\(x=i\\).  \nA non‑vertical line meets the column in at most one point, because two distinct points of the same column would determine a vertical line. Hence the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that lie in column \\(i\\) and are not covered by a vertical line is at most \\(c_i\\).\n\nThe column \\(i\\) contains \\(n+1-i\\) points. If \\(i\\le n-1\\) then \\(n+1-i\\ge 2\\). Therefore we must have \\(c_i\\ge 1\\) for every \\(i\\le n-1\\); otherwise at least two points of that column would remain uncovered. Consequently every column \\(i\\le n-1\\) contains a vertical line, i.e. a non‑sunny line. Hence the number of non‑sunny lines is at least \\(n-1\\). Since the total number of lines is \\(n\\), the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-(n-1)=1 .\n\\]\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge 4\\) we have \\(k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the same argument gives the weaker bound \\(k\\le 3\\); the three‑column case will be examined separately.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Existence for the admissible values  \n\n* **\\(k=0\\) (all \\(n\\) lines non‑sunny).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   They are distinct, none is sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(x=a\\), so the covering condition holds.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line, any \\(n\\ge 3\\)).**  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\; y = x-(n-1).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L\\) passes through the unique point \\((n,1)\\) that is not on any of the vertical lines; all other points have \\(a\\le n-1\\) and are therefore covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\). Hence the covering condition holds and exactly one line (namely \\(L\\)) is sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\) for \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\; y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12 ,\\qquad   \n   L_2:\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 ,\\qquad   \n   L_3:\\; y = x\n   \\]\n\n   are all sunny (their slopes are \\(\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac12,1\\)). A direct verification shows that each of the six points of \\(S_3\\) lies on one of them, so \\(k=3\\) is attainable when \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non‑existence of other values  \n\n*For \\(n\\ge 4\\).*  \nAssume a configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines existed. By the argument of §1, at most one column can lack a vertical line; therefore at most one sunny line can be present. Hence \\(k\\ge 2\\) is impossible.\n\n*For \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe set \\(S_3\\) consists of six points. A direct inspection (or a short case‑by‑case check) shows that the only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(0,1,3\\). The case \\(k=2