                "content": "### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); i.e. it is neither horizontal, nor vertical, nor of the form \\(x+y=c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nFor a fixed column \\(x=i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote  \n\n\\[\nc_i:=\\#\\{\\text{non‑vertical (i.e. sunny) lines that intersect the column }x=i\\}.\n\\]\n\nA non‑vertical line meets a given vertical line \\(x=i\\) in **at most one** point; otherwise the two intersection points would determine a vertical line, contradicting the assumption that the line is non‑vertical.  \nHence a column that contains \\(m\\) points of \\(S_n\\) can be covered by\n\n* one vertical line (a non‑sunny line), or  \n* at most \\(c_i\\) sunny lines, each supplying at most one of those \\(m\\) points.\n\nConsequently, if the column \\(x=i\\) does **not** contain a vertical line, we must have  \n\n\\[\nc_i\\ge m,\n\\qquad\\text{where }m=n+1-i\\text{ is the number of points of }S_n\\text{ in that column.} \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNow observe the sizes of the columns:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\ni & \\#\\text{points in column }x=i \\\\ \\hline\n1 & n\\\\\n2 & n-1\\\\\n\\vdots & \\vdots\\\\\nn-1 & 2\\\\\nn & 1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nIf a column \\(x=i\\) with \\(i\\le n-1\\) lacked a vertical line, then by (1) we would need at least \\(n+1-i\\ge 2\\) sunny lines just for that column.  \nSince a sunny line can cover **at most one** point in a given column, the total number \\(k\\) of sunny lines would have to satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge n+1-i\\ge 2 .\n\\]\n\nThus, **for any configuration with at most one sunny line (\\(k\\le 1\\)) every column \\(x=i\\) with \\(i\\le n-1\\) must contain a vertical line**.  \nOnly the last column \\(x=n\\) (which contains a single point) may be left without a vertical line.\n\nTherefore, for \\(n\\ge 4\\) we obtain the bound  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the same reasoning only yields \\(k\\le 3\\); the case \\(n=3\\) will be examined separately.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Existence for the admissible values  \n\n* **\\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny, any \\(n\\ge 3\\)).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n  \\]\n\n  They are distinct, none is sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(x=a\\); hence all points are covered.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line, any \\(n\\ge 3\\)).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-1)\n  \\]\n\n  together with the sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\; y = x-(n-1).\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\n  *Verification of (3).*  \n  Substituting \\((n,1)\\) gives \\(1=n-(n-1)\\), so \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L\\).  \n  For any other point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a\\le n-1\\); then \\(b=a-(n-1)\\le 0\\), contradicting \\(b>0\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) cannot satisfy (3). Consequently \\((n,1)\\) is the **only** point of \\(S_n\\) on \\(L\\).  \n  All remaining points have \\(a\\le n-1\\) and are therefore covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n  The slope of \\(L\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(L\\) is sunny and exactly one sunny line appears.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\) for \\(n=3\\).**  \n  The following three sunny lines cover all six points of \\(S_3\\):\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: \\; y = x,                         &&\\text{slope }1,\\\\\n  L_2 &: \\; y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,    &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\n  L_3 &: \\; y = -2x + 5,                   &&\\text{slope }-2 .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n  A direct check shows:\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{point} & \\text{line containing it} \\\\ \\hline\n  (1,1) & L_1\\\\\n  (2,2) & L_1\\\\\n  (1,2) & L_2\\\\\n  (3,1) & L_2\\\\\n  (1,3) & L_3\\\\\n  (2,1) & L_3\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  All slopes in (4) are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each line is sunny. Thus \\(k=3\\) is attainable when \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non‑existence of other values  \n\n*For \\(n\\ge 4\\).*  \nAssume a configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines existed.  \nBy the argument of §1, every column \\(x=i\\) with \\(i\\le n-1\\) must contain a vertical line; otherwise that column would require at least two sunny lines, contradicting the assumption that only \\(k\\) sunny lines are available with \\(k\\ge 2\\) but \\(k\\) is already used elsewhere.  \nSince there are \\(n-1\\) such columns, we would need at least \\(n-1\\) vertical lines, leaving at most one line for the remaining columns. This forces \\(k\\le 1\\), a contradiction. Hence for \\(n\\ge 4\\) the only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).\n\n*For \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points listed in the table above.  \nA sunny line can contain at most **two** of these points, because any three collinear points from \\(S_3\\) lie on a line of slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (horizontal, vertical or anti‑diagonal), which are non‑sunny.  \nConsequently, with exactly two sunny lines we could cover at most four points, leaving two points uncovered.  \nThe only lines that can cover two points of \\(S_3\\) are the three non‑sunny types (vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\)). Adding such a non‑sunny line would increase the total number of lines to four, exceeding the allowed number \\(n=3\\).  \nHence a configuration with \\(k=2\\) sunny lines is impossible.  \nTogether with the constructions of §2 we obtain that for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\},   & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
